WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.889
Values that merge showed how a chosen
data type can resolve concurrent writes;

2
00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:06.521
now watch one merge in a set.

3
00:00:07.521 --> 00:00:11.723
A plain write uses last-writer-wins:
if two clusters write the same key,

4
00:00:11.723 --> 00:00:15.691
the later timestamp keeps its value
and silently replaces the other.

5
00:00:16.041 --> 00:00:17.763
A G-Set has a different rule.

6
00:00:17.763 --> 00:00:21.384
It only grows: you can add an element,
but cannot remove one.

7
00:00:21.384 --> 00:00:22.809
Start with an empty set.

8
00:00:22.809 --> 00:00:27.262
Cluster A adds alice while cluster B,
without seeing that change, adds bob.

9
00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:31.358
Their sets are {alice} and {bob};
neither contains the other.

10
00:00:31.358 --> 00:00:36.026
When they merge, each takes the union,
keeping every element from both sets.

11
00:00:36.376 --> 00:00:37.998
Now deliver bob's add again.

12
00:00:37.998 --> 00:00:42.341
Both clusters hold {alice, bob};
the union gives the same result regardless

13
00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:46.395
of delivery order, and repeating
an add does not duplicate an element.

14
00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:50.681
Set union is commutative,
associative and idempotent: swapping,

15
00:00:50.681 --> 00:00:54.430
regrouping or repeating a merge
leaves the result unchanged.

16
00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:58.054
That is why both sides converge
without choosing a winner.

17
00:00:58.404 --> 00:01:03.749
Use a G-Set for append-only membership,
such as seen identifiers or tags.

18
00:01:03.749 --> 00:01:08.434
If your data needs removals,
choose an OR-Set or RW-Set instead;

19
00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:10.704
those define removal semantics.

20
00:01:11.054 --> 00:01:14.106
Back on Build, next:
Scans, filters and cursors.

21
00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:17.794
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
